# Clean Code — Functions

## FN-01 — Keep functions small

Functions should be as short as possible. Martin's rule of thumb: rarely more than twenty lines, ideally fewer. If a function spans multiple screens, it is doing too much.

## FN-02 — Do one thing

A function must perform exactly one logical task and do it well. If you can extract a meaningful sub-function from it, the original function was doing more than one thing.

## FN-03 — Use descriptive names

The name should fully describe what the function does. A long, clear name is better than a short, cryptic one.

```
// bad
function process(d)

// good
function calculateDaysBetweenDates(startDate, endDate)
```

## FN-04 — Prefer fewer arguments

Aim for zero to two arguments. Three is acceptable when clearly justified. More than three almost always indicates that some arguments belong together in a cohesive object.

```
// bad
function createUser(firstName, lastName, email, role, isActive)

// good
function createUser(userDetails)
  // userDetails groups related data
end function
```

## FN-05 — Have no side effects

A function that says it does one thing should not secretly modify external state, mutate its inputs, or produce observable changes beyond its return value.

```
// bad — name implies only validation, but also initialises a session
function checkPassword(username, password)
  if isValid(username, password)
    Session.initialise()  // hidden side effect
    return true
  end if
  return false
end function

// good — separate the concerns
function isPasswordValid(username, password)
  return isValid(username, password)
end function
```

## FN-06 — Never use flag arguments

Passing a boolean to change a function's behaviour means the function does two things. Split it into two separate functions.

```
// bad
function render(page, isSuite)

// good
function renderPage(page)
function renderPageWithSetups(page)
```
